      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Given that the quadratic equation \\(ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0\\) has two roots \\(3\\) and \\(-2\\), a third root must be a linear combination of these roots. Since adding the roots of the polynomial does not change the value of \\(\\frac{b+c}{a}\\), the third root must also be \\(3 + (-2) = 1\\).\n\nThus, the polynomial has roots \\(3\\), \\(-2\\), and \\(1\\). Using the fact that the sum of the roots of the polynomial \\(ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0\\) is given by \\(-\\frac{b}{a}\\), we can find \\(b\\) as follows:\n\\[\n3 + (-2) + 1 = 2 \\implies 3 - 2 + 1 = 2 \\implies 2 = -\\frac{b}{a} \\implies b = -2a.\n\\]\nNext, using the product of the roots, we get:\n\\[\n3(-2)(1) = -6 \\implies 3 \\cdot -2 \\cdot 1 = -6 \\implies -6 = -\\frac{d}{a} \\implies d = 6a.\n\\]\nWe also know that the sum of the product of the roots taken two at a time is given by \\(\\frac{c}{a}\\):\n\\[\n3(-2) + 3(1) + (-2)(1) = -6 + 3 - 2 = -5 \\implies 3 \\cdot -2 + 3 \\cdot 1 + (-2) \\cdot 1 = -5 \\implies -6 + 3 - 2 = -5 \\implies -5 = \\frac{c}{a} \\implies c = -5a.\n\\]\nNow we can find \\(\\frac{b+c}{a}\\):\n\\[\nb + c = -2a - 5a = -7a \\implies \\frac{b+c}{a} = \\frac{-7a}{a} = -7.\n\\]\nThus, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-7}.\n\\]",
