      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given that two of the roots of the cubic equation \\( ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0 \\) are \\( 3 \\) and \\( -2 \\),we can use the properties of polynomial roots and coefficients to find \\(\\frac{b+c}{a}\\).\n\nFirst, let's denote the roots of the polynomial as \\( r_1, r_2, \\) and \\( r_3 \\). Here, \\( r_1 = 3 \\) and \\( r_2 = -2 \\).\n\nFor a cubic polynomial \\( ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0 \\), the sum of the roots (by Vieta's formulas) is given by:\n\\[\nr_1 + r_2 + r_3 = -\\frac{b}{a}\n\\]\nSubstituting the known roots:\n\\[\n3 + (-2) + r_3 = -\\frac{b}{a}\n\\]\nSimplifying the left-hand side:\n\\[\n1 + r_3 = -\\frac{b}{a}\n\\]\nThus, we have:\n\\[\nr_3 = -\\frac{b}{a} - 1\n\\]\n\nNext, consider the product of the roots taken one at a time (also by Vieta's formulas):\n\\[\nr_1 r_2 r_3 = -\\frac{d}{a}\n\\]\nSubstituting the known roots:\n\\[\n3 \\cdot (-2) \\cdot r_3 = -\\frac{d}{a}\n\\]\nSimplifying the left-hand side:\n\\[\n-6r_3 = -\\frac{d}{a}\n\\]\nThus, we have:\n\\[\nd = 6r_3 a\n\\]\n\nWe also know that the sum of the product of the roots taken two at a time is given by:\n\\[\nr_1 r_2 + r_2 r_3 + r_3 r_1 = \\frac{c}{a}\n\\]\nSubstituting the known roots:\n\\[\n3 \\cdot (-2) + (-2) \\cdot r_3 + r_3 \\cdot 3 = \\frac{c}{a}\n\\]\nSimplifying the left-hand side:\n\\[\n-6 + (-2r_3) + 3r_3 = \\frac{c}{a}\n\\]\n\\[\n-6 + r_3 = \\frac{c}{a}\n\\]\nThus, we have:\n\\[\nr_3 = \\frac{c}{a} + 6\n\\]\n\nSince both expressions for \\( r_3 \\) must be equal, we set them equal to each other:\n\\[\n-\\frac{b}{a} - 1 = \\frac{c}{a} + 6\n\\]\nMultiplying through by \\( a \\) to clear the denominator:\n\\[\n-b - a = c + 6a\n\\]\nRearranging terms to solve for \\( b + c \\):\n\\[\n-b - c = 6a + a\n\\]\n\\[\n-b - c = 7a\n\\]\n\\[\nb + c = -7a\n\\]\n\nFinally, we find \\(\\frac{b + c}{a}\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{b + c}{a} = \\frac{-7a}{a} = -7\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-7}\n\\]",
