      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Given that two of the roots of the polynomial \\( ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0 \\) are \\( 3 \\) and \\( -2 \\), we can express the polynomial in its factored form as:\n\n\\[ a(x - 3)(x + 2)(x - r) \\]\n\nwhere \\( r \\) is the third root of the polynomial. Expanding this product step by step, we start with the inner products:\n\n\\[ (x - 3)(x + 2) = x^2 + 2x - 3x - 6 = x^2 - x - 6 \\]\n\nNext, we multiply this result by \\( (x - r) \\):\n\n\\[ (x^2 - x - 6)(x - r) = x^3 - rx^2 - x^2 + rx - 6x + 6r = x^3 - (r+1)x^2 + (r-6)x + 6r \\]\n\nThus, the polynomial can be written as:\n\n\\[ a(x^3 - (r+1)x^2 + (r-6)x + 6r) = ax^3 - a(r+1)x^2 + a(r-6)x + 6ar \\]\n\nBy comparing the coefficients of the expanded form with the standard form \\( ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d \\), we identify the coefficients:\n\\[ b = -a(r+1) \\]\n\\[ c = a(r-6) \\]\n\\[ d = 6ar \\]\n\nWe need to find \\( \\frac{b+c}{a} \\). First, we sum \\( b \\) and \\( c \\):\n\n\\[ b + c = -a(r+1) + a(r-6) = -a(r+1) + ar - 6a = -ar - a + ar - 6a = -7a \\]\n\nThen, we compute the ratio:\n\n\\[ \\frac{b+c}{a} = \\frac{-7a}{a} = -7 \\]\n\nThus, the value is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{-7} \\]",
